[0x02] FROM 2600 Hz CAP'N CRUNCH WHISTLES TO 2-SLOT TDMA DMR
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Back in the golden age of 90s phreaking, seizing an AT&T long-distance trunk
took a plastic Bo'sun whistle from a box of Cap'n Crunch cereal resonating at
exactly 2600 Hz, followed by KP + number + ST in multi-frequency (MF) tones
from a home-brewed Blue Box.

Today, amateur radio operators are the legitimate heirs to the phreaking craft:
  * Instead of KP/ST MF tones, we sling CTCSS sub-audible PL tones (71.9 Hz,
    100.0 Hz, 123.0 Hz, 156.7 Hz) to wake up analog repeaters across Augusta.
  * Instead of seizing Trunk #1, we slice a 12.5 kHz UHF channel into two
    30-millisecond TDMA Timeslots (TS1 & TS2) with 4-FSK modulation at 4800
    symbols/sec (9600 bps) and dial Talkgroup 358 (CSRA Talk) on the TGIF
    network.
  * Instead of an acoustic coupler strapped to a payphone with alligator clips
    (the classic Beige Box), we speak raw hexadecimal over USB CDC ACM
    (28e9:018a) and WCH CH340 UART bridges.

[0x03] THE BOOK OF ARMAMENTS: PATCHING DMRCONFIG FOR D6X2UV2
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
And Saint Attila raised the BTECH DMR-6X2 PRO up on high, saying:
"O Lord, bless this thy 1,606,528-byte codeplug image, that with it thou mayst
blow thine unprogrammed channels to tiny bits, in thy mercy."

When we cracked open the raw hex dump of the BTECH DMR-6X2 PRO (`D6X2UV2`,
firmware `24040800D0060110`), we discovered why unpatched `dmrconfig` threw
165 bogus `Channel X: scanlist 1 not found` errors:
  1. The BTECH DMR-6X2 family stores EIGHT scanlist indices per channel
     starting at `ch->aprs_channel`.
  2. The AnyTone AT-D878UVII (`D878UV2`) stores ONE scanlist index at
     `ch->scan_list_index` (offset +0x22).
  3. Stock `radio.c` mapped `"D6X2UV2"` to `&radio_d878uv2`, while
     `anytone_ht.c` only checked `if (radio == &radio_dmr6x2)`.
  4. By forging `radio_dmr6x2pro` in `vendor/dmrconfig/{radio.h,radio.c,anytone_ht.c}`,
     round-trip binary compilation achieved 100% bit-level purity!

